From: v0id <tyler@slaughter.pro>
Subject: window: two-pass rule evaluation to allow cross-property is-floating/is-focused matching

Currently is_floating() and is_focused() always return false for unmapped windows,
which prevents any use of `match is-floating=true` or `match is-focused=true` in
window rules that run at initial configure time.

The original concern was that `match is-floating=true { open-floating true }` creates
a cycle: is_floating depends on open_floating, which depends on is_floating.

This patch narrows the restriction to only the same-property pair that actually causes
the cycle. It adds a preliminary pass over the rules (using the current safe defaults
of is_floating=false and is_focused=false), then in the main pass:

- Rules that set open_floating still see is_floating=false (no cycle possible).
- Rules that do NOT set open_floating see is_floating=prelim_open_floating,
  enabling e.g. `match is-floating=true { open-focused true }`.
- Symmetric treatment for is_focused / open_focused.

The preliminary pass fully replicates compute_open_floating()'s logic, including the
parent-window and fixed-height heuristics. It cannot call compute_open_floating()
directly because that function calls with_states() internally, and with_toplevel_role()
holds the same Smithay surface lock — calling it inside would deadlock. Instead,
toplevel.parent() and the SurfaceCachedState min/max sizes are read BEFORE entering
with_toplevel_role(), stored in unmapped_heuristic, and used inside the closure.

Example now supported:
  window-rule {
      match is-floating=true
      open-focused true
  }

